# API Design

## Objective

Public interfaces  -  REST, RPC, SDK exports, module headers  -  should be intuitive and consistent. Predictability beats personal preference.

> **If this project has no HTTP/REST API surface:** apply the same principles to the nearest public interface (module APIs, SDK callbacks, CLI commands, RPC, message contracts) or skip.

## Conventions

- Follow existing naming, auth, request/response, and error patterns.
- Extend conventions; do not invent parallel styles.

## REST (when applicable)

- Resource-oriented routes; correct HTTP methods and status codes.
- Consistent response envelope for success and errors.
- Validate all input; authenticate and authorize explicitly.
- Pagination, filtering, sorting for collections.
- Version breaking changes; preserve backward compatibility when practical.
- Idempotent PUT; GET must not mutate state.
- Errors helpful to consumers, not leaky internals.

## Documentation

Update documentation in `/docs` when the change is significant (see `documentation-first.mdc`).  -  endpoints, contracts, auth, validation, examples.

